          "start_ms": null,
          "status": "needs-review",
          "text": "Description: On July 26, 2009, at approximately 10:20 PM, a witness observing the starry sky from their terrace spots a bright white light moving in a linear S-N trajectory at an elevation of 25° to 35°. The witness initially follows the light for 7 to 8 seconds, mistaking it for a satellite. After briefly looking away for one to two seconds, the witness is surprised to no longer see the light on its trajectory. However, they spot a bright light making a turn towards the west for 2 to 3 seconds (in the direction of the Habsheim airport). The light then takes off on a high-speed linear trajectory passing directly overhead. No unusual noises are heard during the 10 to 12-second observation, which ends at 11:48 PM. The witness remains puzzled by the overall trajectory of the light, including a change in direction and the significant acceleration of the phenomenon without any sounds. While nothing confirms that it was a single light source, several hypotheses are ruled out (as detailed in the investigation report): aircraft passage (no radar traces in the Rixsheim area during the 11:30 PM to midnight time slot), celestial bodies (Jupiter was too low on the horizon), and satellite passage (the COSMOS 1151-Rocket passed overhead, but at a 57° elevation). Despite not being particularly strange, the phenomenon remains unexplained. The D1 classification is justified by the uniqueness of the testimony and the impossibility of estimating the distance to the phenomenon.",
          "translation_text_sha256": "c954140400c4b01cd1f670da89a2d9e630a119de08b1b979c99b260eb55820a7"
        }
      ],
